+Chance level here is 50 per cent. The best model, SVM-A with absolute power and a linear
+kernel, reached 53.9 per cent and was above chance for 35 of the 50 patients. Its
+p-value of 0.008 does not survive a correction for the 16 settings that were tried, so even
+on the stroke data the models are only slightly better than guessing. Adding the power
+ratio in SVM-B did not help, and the ratio on its own in SVM-C was at chance.
+
+The models were then trained on all the stroke recordings and tested on the 72 usable
+trials recorded with the GELO headset. Table~\ref{tab:svm_recorded} gives the results.
+Because there are 50 right-hand and 22 left-hand trials, a model that always answered
+``right'' would score 50/72, or 69.4 per cent, without having learned anything. Plain
+accuracy is therefore misleading here, and the table also gives the balanced accuracy (the
+average of the two per-class recalls), the recall for each hand separately, and the
+p-value of a permutation test with shuffled labels.
