+None of the twelve combinations learned to tell the hands apart. The balanced accuracy
+lies between 45.5 and 53.4 per cent, and every permutation p-value is 0.38 or higher, so
+none of the results is better than chance. The models using absolute power look
+reasonable on plain accuracy, 60 to 69 per cent, but only because they answer ``right''
+for almost every trial: their right-hand recall is 82 to 100 per cent while their
+left-hand recall is 0 to 9 per cent. SVM-C with absolute power predicted ``right'' for
+every single trial, which is exactly how it scores the 69.4 per cent majority baseline.
+The models using ERD power split their answers more evenly, but they are still at chance.
+Section~\ref{sec:bci2b_recorded} reports the matching test for the 2b-trained model on a
+per-file basis, where the 15 recorded files give 15 independent decisions, with the same
+outcome.
 
